---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BlurFaceInPhoto from '@/components/tools/image-edit/BlurFaceInPhoto.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="blur-license-plate" width="wide">
  <BlurFaceInPhoto client:load defaultEffect="block" defaultShape="rect" subject="plate" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to hide a number plate</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the photos of the car on the page. Each photo is checked for plates, and every plate gets a box.</li>
      <li>Check each photo. Click a box to keep it visible if it is not a plate. Drag on the photo to add a box the model missed.</li>
      <li>Keep the solid fill. It is the safe choice for text such as a plate.</li>
      <li>Download one photo, or all photos as one ZIP file. The GPS tag and the rest of the metadata are removed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The plate model</h2>
    <p>
      The plates are found by an object detection model trained on licence plates (16 MB, MIT license). It runs in your
      browser in a model runtime of 15 MB. Both files download once from this site and are kept in your browser, so the
      tool works offline after the first use.
    </p>
    <p>
      The model checks the whole photo, and large photos also in overlapping tiles, so plates on cars further away are
      found too. In our tests with European, UK, US and Georgian plates it found about 4 of 5 plates. It sometimes marks
      a sticker or a dealer badge. A photo takes one to three seconds on a laptop.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a solid fill and not a blur</h2>
    <p>
      A plate has few characters from a known set, in a known font. Someone can render every possible plate, blur it the
      same way and compare it with your photo. A solid fill leaves nothing to compare. Use blur for faces and a fill for
      plates.
    </p>

    <h2>How to check that nothing is uploaded</h2>
    <p>
      Open the developer tools of your browser (F12), select the Network tab and drop a photo. You see the model files
      load from this site the first time and no request with your photo. You can also load the tool once, turn off your
      network and use it.
    </p>

    <h2>What else can identify the car</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The GPS tag in the file. It is removed when you save from this page.</li>
      <li>A house number or a street sign. Add a box over it.</li>
      <li>The VIN behind the windscreen, parking permits and dealer stickers. Add a box over them.</li>
      <li>The plate in another photo of the same listing. Drop all photos together and check each one.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Small, dirty, bent or partly hidden plates can be missed. Look at every photo before you share it.</li>
      <li>Plates on motorbikes and plates in unusual formats are found less often.</li>
      <li>Plates seen at a steep angle get a box that is a little too large. That is safe, but you can redraw it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/blur-face-in-photo">Blur Face in Photo</a> for people in the same photo,
      <a href="/censor-image">Censor Image</a> for documents and screenshots, and
      <a href="/compress-image-to-500kb">Compress to 500 KB</a> when a listing site limits the file size.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
